<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhu Zaiyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a>–<a href="/years/1611">1611</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhu Zaiyu was a Chinese scholar, mathematician and music theorist, a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, who in 1584 described equal temperament by accurate mathematical calculation. Born in Qinyang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, a sixth-generation descendant of the Hongxi Emperor, he inherited the title of Prince of Zheng in 1593 but soon gave it up to his cousin. He wrote treatises on music theory and history, dance, astronomy, mathematics and the calendar, calculated the magnetic declination of Peking and the length of the tropical year. His work on equal temperament won no official recognition under the Ming or the Qing, which favoured classical textual scholarship over empirical observation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some turned to learning: <b>Zhu Zaiyu</b>, a sixth-generation descendant of the Hongxi Emperor, discovered equal temperament in music.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
